page.operations:
When is SpringSaLaD the right tool? Use it when the answer depends on both the sizes and locations of molecular species:
when excluded volume matters - crowding, or a site shielded by the rest of its own molecule;
when the membrane tether matters - a kinase that can only reach a substrate it is anchored near;
when the question is about complexes and aggregates - cluster sizes, how many molecules end up in the largest aggregate, how that changes with valency or during signaling;
When it is not. Every particle is tracked individually and every pair is tested for collision, so simulation time grows quickly with particle number. A model of tens of thousands of molecules, or one that has to run for minutes of simulated time, belongs in a network-free or deterministic application instead. Nothing is lost by trying the less computationally costly approaches: they are all Applications based on the same Physiology.
Where to start. Create a Physiology with 3 Structures: Extracellular, Membrane and Intracellular. Build the molecules and rules containing reaction types supported by SpringSaLaD; then create a SpringSaLaD Application. It supplies the geometry itself, and its Molecular Structures tab is where the molecular structure detail goes.
